"""Original evidence-aware prevention and caregiver-support learning."""
def build(c, m):
    return c(69, 'Preventing Violence and Supporting Caregivers', 'Preventing Violence\nand Supporting\nCaregivers',
        'Develop a bounded fictional prevention proposal that considers caregiving, structural barriers, suitable support and outcome-specific evidence without delivering a clinical or parenting programme.', [
        m('Prevention across family community and service settings', 'Identify complementary prevention responsibilities without blaming children or caregivers for structural barriers.', [
            'Prevention includes reducing opportunities for harm, strengthening supportive relationships and addressing barriers around families. A narrow plan that asks a caregiver to change while ignoring inaccessible services or unsafe programme conditions may miss important responsibilities. The CPMS socio-ecological approach considers children, families, communities and wider systems together. Use this as a way to organise questions in a fictional proposal, not as a causal scoring instrument. Describe the concern, the relevant level of action and the authority or resources needed before claiming that an intervention can be delivered. [S42]',
            'Caregiver support and child protection can be complementary, but support must not be confused with a finding about an individual family. Poverty, disability, distress or displacement does not by itself establish maltreatment or justify removing a child. An invented case may identify a practical service barrier without diagnosing a caregiver or predicting abuse. Keep the focus on suitable access, respectful engagement and qualified responsibility. The existing CPS courses address family preservation and permanency; this addition examines prevention planning and evidence rather than repeating placement decisions or granting a new statutory role. [S42, S41]',
            'A review of parenting interventions in humanitarian settings found promising evidence for some outcomes, with substantial variation across interventions and settings. Its search was updated in 2022, despite the paper appearing in a 2025 journal issue. This distinction matters when a proposal describes evidence as current. The research supports considering suitable parenting support within a wider response; it does not show that any generic workshop will prevent every form of violence. A learner should ask about the population, outcome, delivery capacity and limits of the evidence rather than attach a broad effectiveness label to a new activity. [R52]',
            'Prevention messages should avoid implying that children are responsible for stopping adult harm or that a caregiver must disclose distress to deserve ordinary support. Offer clear, accessible information about suitable qualified arrangements within the actual setting. The course uses invented examples only and does not recruit families, teach a programme manual or provide individual clinical advice. A strong fictional proposal can identify a support need, an appropriate responsible service and an unresolved access condition. It should remain honest about what the learner knows, what requires confirmation and what is outside their role. [S41, S42]'],
            'A fictional prevention proposal blames missed appointments entirely on caregivers, although the service is inaccessible and transport is uncertain. It promises that a single generic workshop will prevent all violence.',
            'Rewrite the proposal’s rationale to identify family, organisational and structural responsibilities, a suitable support objective and the evidence limits of the promised effect.',
            'Address service and access conditions alongside caregiver support. Replace the universal promise with a specific conditional objective and identify the responsible qualified arrangements.', ['S42', 'S41', 'R52'], [
                ('Prevention planning should consider more than individual behaviour.', 'A fictional programme ignores an inaccessible service and attributes missed visits only to caregiver motivation. What should its review add?', 'The service barrier, organisational responsibilities and feasible access support.', ['A finding of maltreatment from missed visits alone.', 'A rule that access barriers are irrelevant.', 'A guarantee that one workshop resolves every cause of harm.'], 'A wider prevention analysis considers the conditions around families.'),
                ('Adversity alone does not establish an individual maltreatment finding.', 'An invented family experiences poverty and displacement. Which conclusion is justified from that information alone?', 'Support and access needs may deserve attention, but an individual maltreatment finding is not established.', ['The caregiver necessarily harms the child.', 'A learner should independently order separation.', 'No further contextual understanding is needed.'], 'A risk context is not a diagnosis, legal finding or inevitable outcome.'),
                ('Evidence claims must identify the relevant intervention and outcome.', 'A fictional grant note says any parenting workshop prevents all forms of violence. What is the main correction?', 'Use outcome-specific evidence and examine population, delivery conditions and uncertainty.', ['Keep the universal claim because parenting is important.', 'Replace evidence with the workshop title.', 'Assume every prevention activity has identical effects.'], 'Evidence for some programmes and outcomes cannot validate every activity.'),
                ('Publication year differs from a review’s search date.', 'An invented evidence note calls the Backhaus review a search of all studies through 2025. What needs correction?', 'Its journal issue is 2025, but the reported search update was August 2022.', ['Every article’s search necessarily ends in its issue year.', 'The review was a Nigerian field experiment in 2025.', 'A later website upload changes the underlying search period.'], 'The appraisal must distinguish publication, search and fieldwork dates.'),
                ('Support should not require unnecessary personal disclosure.', 'A fictional information session makes caregiver access conditional on describing family trauma in class. What should change?', 'Provide a suitable non-disclosive way to learn and access information.', ['Reward the most detailed personal history.', 'Require children to validate caregivers’ accounts.', 'Collect private case records to prove eligibility for ordinary course information.'], 'Introductory learning does not require sensitive family disclosures.')]),
        m('Support options access and qualified delivery', 'Review an invented caregiver-support option for suitability, access and competence dependencies.', [
            'A support option needs a clear purpose and a suitable delivery arrangement. Describe the intended population, the specific concern and the type of support that a responsible qualified service can provide. An introductory learner should not infer competence to deliver a structured intervention from reading about its research. The CPMS caregiving and workforce guidance supports suitable help and competent staff, while KCS guidance keeps organisational responsibility visible. In a fictional plan, identify who would need to confirm suitability, what training or supervision is required and which elements remain outside the learner’s role. [S42, S41]',
            'Recent research can inform these questions without becoming a treatment manual. A 2026 pragmatic trial with displaced and migrant caregivers on the Thailand-Myanmar border reported benefits in some caregiver and parenting outcomes after an integrated intervention. Its findings did not show improvement in every child outcome. The setting, participant selection, trained delivery and follow-up conditions matter. The course therefore uses the study to ask about contextual fit and outcome distinctions; it does not reproduce its sessions, therapeutic techniques, questionnaires or training requirements, or predict a similar effect in a Nigerian programme. [R54]',
            'Access should be considered alongside content. Timing, transport, language, disability, caring responsibilities, cost and fear may affect participation. Avoid interpreting non-attendance as proof that a family does not care. An appropriate organisation may need to offer alternatives or link people to suitable support, subject to actual service availability and local safeguards. In the invented scenario, distinguish a confirmed resource from one assumed for planning. A respectful plan should not force a caregiver to disclose violence publicly, promise absolute confidentiality or offer specialist treatment through an unqualified worker. [S41, S42]',
            'Support arrangements also need boundaries when concerns exceed the programme’s scope. A caregiver education activity is not a substitute for a qualified response to an actual protection concern or emergency. Identify the relevant designated route and local decision responsibility rather than invent a universal referral rule. Keep the child’s safety, views and appropriate communication in the analysis without asking a real child to supply evidence for coursework. The learner’s output is a conditional fictional support plan, not a completed intervention, clinical judgement or proof of professional competence. [S42, S41]'],
            'An invented project copies the title of a published intervention, assigns delivery to an untrained learner and calls non-attendance a lack of care. It assumes specialist support is available without checking.',
            'Prepare a suitability-and-access note identifying the actual objective, qualified delivery dependencies, two access barriers and a conditional support route.',
            'Research does not transfer a programme’s competence requirements to the learner. Confirm suitability, delivery support and service availability, address access barriers and preserve a qualified response for concerns outside scope.', ['S42', 'S41', 'R54'], [
                ('Reading a trial does not confer intervention-delivery competence.', 'A fictional learner is asked to run a specialist programme after reading its abstract. What is appropriate?', 'Identify the relevant training, supervision and authorised delivery requirements before any real implementation.', ['Treat the article title as a provider qualification.', 'Reproduce a questionnaire and begin independent treatment.', 'Promise that a course badge establishes clinical competence.'], 'Evidence appraisal and competent delivery are different responsibilities.'),
                ('A study’s setting and outcome distinctions matter.', 'A fictional proposal cites the 2026 border-setting trial as proof that every child outcome will improve locally. What should be corrected?', 'The transfer and outcome claims; benefits were not shown for every child outcome or every setting.', ['Only the citation’s font size.', 'The need to consider training and local suitability.', 'The fact that it was a randomised study.'], 'A trial supports bounded conclusions about its actual setting and outcomes.'),
                ('Non-attendance may reflect practical barriers.', 'An invented caregiver misses a session because transport was not available. What should be examined?', 'Access conditions and feasible alternatives rather than a conclusion that the caregiver does not care.', ['Automatic proof of poor motivation.', 'A requirement for public trauma disclosure before assistance.', 'An invented diagnosis explaining the absence.'], 'Participation depends on circumstances as well as individual intentions.'),
                ('Unverified support must remain conditional.', 'A fictional plan lists a specialist service but no one has confirmed capacity. Which wording is accurate?', 'Potential support, subject to confirmation by the responsible role.', ['Guaranteed treatment already delivered.', 'A completed referral and verified recovery.', 'A promise that the learner can replace the unavailable specialist.'], 'A proposed resource is distinct from confirmed access and actual service delivery.'),
                ('Routine support cannot replace a qualified protection response.', 'An invented education session encounters a concern outside its agreed scope. What boundary applies?', 'Use the designated qualified arrangements for the actual setting rather than improvising independent casework.', ['Continue the ordinary lesson as the only response.', 'Ask the class to investigate the concern.', 'Guarantee resolution through a generic parenting message.'], 'Programme scope must not obscure a need for appropriate qualified action.')]),
        m('Review outcomes and avoid causal overclaim', 'Distinguish activities, self-reported change and defensible outcome conclusions in an invented prevention review.', [
            'A prevention review should distinguish what was delivered from what changed. Attendance, completed sessions and distributed handouts describe activities or outputs; they do not by themselves demonstrate a reduction in violence. Define the intended outcome, the period and the evidence needed to assess it through an appropriately governed evaluation. In this introductory course, use invented aggregate data only. The task is to interpret a fictional report accurately rather than design an advanced causal study, collect real family histories or administer a source research instrument. [S42, R52]',
            'Associations observed at one time do not establish the sequence or mechanism of change. A 2025 study of Eastern European mothers found cross-sectional associations relevant to family violence, but its proposed pathways did not replicate in the longitudinal analysis. Intervention exposure, attrition and pandemic timing complicate interpretation. This is a reason to avoid a deterministic explanation or blame directed at caregivers or children. Use the example to distinguish a plausible question from an established causal finding; it does not supply a diagnosis or a local risk prediction for an individual family. [R53]',
            'Outcome measures and context also shape what a programme can claim. The 2026 border-setting trial relied on self-reported outcomes and included relatively few male caregivers. Its caregiver and parenting findings cannot establish that all children’s emotional or behavioural difficulties improved, or that the effect will persist indefinitely. The humanitarian meta-analysis also found heterogeneous effects and little longer-term evidence. A responsible fictional review should describe the relevant findings, uncertainty and transfer limits rather than combine unlike outcomes into a universal success statement. Do not use a journal’s rank as a substitute for this appraisal. [R54, R52]',
            'Learning from a review can include revising access arrangements, clarifying delivery responsibility or acknowledging that evidence is insufficient. Name the proposed change, responsible owner and review point. Preserve the distinction between a recommendation and an implemented action, and avoid presenting an attractive portfolio as completed field practice. Reading or passing an offline exercise cannot award a course badge or school certificate. Those require the academy’s approved assessment arrangements, verified ParaLearn results and the relevant human review and signatures. The prevention worksheet provides evidence of bounded reasoning, not proof of intervention effectiveness or independent professional competence. [S41, S42]'],
            'An invented project reports forty attendances, improved caregiver confidence and fewer reports to one channel as proof that violence ended and every child’s mental health improved.',
            'Write an evidence review separating activity, self-report, reporting-channel change and child outcomes. Add two limitations and one feasible improvement with a responsible owner.',
            'Attendance and confidence are not proof that violence ended. Examine the measures and reporting access, keep child outcomes distinct and state limitations without inventing causal effects or professional authority.', ['S42', 'R52', 'R53', 'R54', 'S41'], [
                ('Activity counts are not outcome proof.', 'A fictional prevention report counts forty attendances and declares that violence was eliminated. What is justified?', 'The attendance count describes participation, while the claimed protection outcome needs appropriate evidence.', ['Attendance proves that all violence stopped.', 'Every participant necessarily changed behaviour.', 'A larger handout count establishes the same outcome.'], 'Outputs and outcomes answer different questions.'),
                ('Cross-sectional associations do not establish causal pathways.', 'An invented report uses the Bruehl study to claim an inevitable mechanism of harm in every family. What should be corrected?', 'The deterministic causal claim, especially given the non-replicated longitudinal pathways and study limitations.', ['Only the article’s publication year.', 'The need to avoid blaming caregivers.', 'The distinction between a selected sample and every family.'], 'The study does not establish an inevitable or universally causal pathway.'),
                ('Caregiver and child outcomes should remain distinct.', 'A fictional programme improves caregiver confidence and assumes every child’s emotional difficulties improved. What is missing?', 'Relevant child-outcome evidence rather than substitution of a different outcome.', ['A stronger success slogan.', 'An assumption that all measures are interchangeable.', 'A statement that no child measure could ever matter.'], 'Improvement in one domain does not establish improvement in every other domain.'),
                ('Fewer reports can reflect a changed channel.', 'An invented project receives fewer concerns after making its channel harder to access. What cannot be inferred from the count alone?', 'That the underlying incidence of harm decreased.', ['That the recorded number was lower.', 'That access conditions merit review.', 'That changes in recording can affect apparent trends.'], 'A reporting count is not a direct measure of the true incidence of harm.'),
                ('A review recommendation needs a responsible next step.', 'A fictional review identifies transport barriers but lists no follow-up. What improves its usefulness?', 'A feasible proposed adjustment, named responsible role and review point.', ['A claim that identifying the barrier resolved it.', 'An invented clinical outcome.', 'A promise that the worksheet qualifies the learner for independent practice.'], 'An actionable review connects a finding to bounded responsibility and follow-through.')])
    ])
